# Pending human-applied patch — issue #679
#
# WHAT: adds a guard deny rule for raw-git-transport `git push` invocations whose effect can
#       reach the repository's default branch — refspec destinations naming it (bare name,
#       `HEAD:main`, `feature:main`, the `:main` delete form, `refs/heads/main`), `git push
#       --delete <remote> <default>`, `git push --mirror`/`--all` (either can carry the default
#       branch regardless of other args), and `--force`/`-f`/`--force-with-lease` combinations
#       (subsumed by the same refspec-destination check, not special-cased). Active ONLY when
#       the trusted spawn env `SAPWOOD_DEFAULT_BRANCH` is set (worker.ts wires it — see this
#       issue's ordinary-path PR); unset leaves the rule inactive, so non-worker `claude`
#       sessions are unaffected. Also adds guard.test.ts's BLOCK/ALLOW matrix for the rule
#       (below), which the applying human's own `npm test` (post-apply) exercises.
#
# gate② round 1 (codex sol-high) reproduced three EXECUTABLE bypasses of the round-1 shipped
# rule and one over-block; this revision closes all four:
#   (a) `git push origin HEAD:$SAPWOOD_DEFAULT_BRANCH` — the guard sees the literal unexpanded
#       token, but the worker's OWN shell expands the env value to `HEAD:main` before git ever
#       runs. Fixed: ANY refspec argument containing an unresolved shell variable ($FOO/${FOO}/
#       $(...)) or backtick command-substitution is blocked outright — the guard cannot prove
#       such a token is not the default branch, so it does not try.
#   (b) `git -c alias.p=push p origin HEAD:main` — a transient `-c alias.p=push` makes the
#       non-`push` subcommand `p` perform a push, evading the "is the subcommand push" gate.
#       Fixed: any `-c`/`--config` that defines or overrides an `alias.*` key is treated as an
#       OPAQUE git invocation — blocked unconditionally, same doctrine already applied to
#       `eval`/`sh -c` wrappers — since the effective subcommand can no longer be trusted from
#       argv alone.
#   (c) `git push origin 'refs/heads/*:refs/heads/*'` — a wildcard destination that matches the
#       default branch without ever naming it; the prior exact-destination compare missed it.
#       Fixed: any refspec destination containing a `*` wildcard is blocked (git ref names never
#       legitimately contain `*`, so this has no legitimate-branch false-positive).
#   (d) over-block: `git push -o main origin lane-123-abc` and `git push main lane-123-abc`
#       (remote literally named `main`) were blocked because the scan treated push-option VALUES
#       and the remote itself as candidate refspecs. Fixed: value-taking push options
#       (`-o`/`--push-option`, `--receive-pack`, `--exec`, `--repo`) and the remote are now
#       parsed positionally-separately from actual refspecs, so a non-default lane-only push is
#       correctly ALLOWed per the issue's own contract.
# The design frame this revision commits to: "if the guard cannot PROVE a push's destination is
# not the default branch, block it" — rather than enumerating one more literal form each time a
# new bypass surfaces. Regression rows for (a)-(d), plus the issue's own enumerated-but-unpinned
# forms (`refs/heads/main`, `--force`/`--force-with-lease` long forms, and a `git -C dir push`
# row pinning gitSkipGlobalFlags), are in guard.test.ts's hunk below.
#
# gate② round 2 (codex sol-high, "final confirmation") found one more argv-scan-closable gap and
# one that isn't:
#   (7) `git push --repo origin main` / `git push --repo=origin main` — `--repo`/`--repo=` SUPPLY
#       the repository directly; there is no separate positional remote to skip. The round-1
#       parser didn't know this, so it still discarded positional[0] (here, the actual refspec
#       `main`) as if it were an unwritten remote, and never scanned it. Fixed: `parsePushArgs`
#       now tracks whether `--repo`/`--repo=` supplied the repository (`repoFromFlag`) and, when
#       it did, scans EVERY positional as a refspec rather than skipping the first.
#   (8) `git config alias.p push && git p origin HEAD:main` (and the one-shot `GIT_CONFIG_COUNT=1
#       GIT_CONFIG_KEY_0=alias.p GIT_CONFIG_VALUE_0=push git p ...` env form) — NOT fixed, by PM
#       ruling. The malicious intent here lives in git STATE the argv scan cannot see: a
#       PRE-PERSISTED `git config alias.*` (repo-local, set by an EARLIER, separately-judged
#       command) or `GIT_CONFIG_*` environment aliases resolved by a LATER, argv-innocent
#       invocation. `gitHasAliasInjection` only ever sees the CURRENT command's own `-c`/
#       `--config` tokens — extending it to this class would mean modeling git's own config
#       resolution across commands and env, not scanning one more token spelling, which is a
#       design-level question the issue's own text already answers: this rule is engine-side
#       "defense-in-depth," explicitly "never a replacement for" GitHub branch protection (DR
#       #616), the backstop of record for exactly this class. Accepted as a documented residual,
#       the same class checkControlSentinelArg's "hardcoded path in a script" residual already
#       is — see docs/security.md's #679 section for the full paragraph, and guard.test.ts's
#       "KNOWN RESIDUAL" row below, which pins the current (accepted) ALLOW so a future reader
#       sees a decided boundary, not an oversight.
#
# WHY: Category C already enforces producer≠merger at the `gh` layer (`gh pr merge` etc denied).
#       It has no `git push` handling at all, so a worker leg holding `Bash(git *)` — with host
#       credentials at L0, or the #606 L1 deploy key, which docs/security.md itself notes "CAN
#       still `git push` directly to an unprotected default branch, bypassing the review gate
#       entirely via raw git transport" — can run `git push origin HEAD:<default-branch>`: a
#       merge-equivalent that skips gate①/gate② on any repo without branch protection. Branch
#       protection remains the backstop of record (DR #616) — this is engine-side defense-in-
#       depth AT the guard's own sanctioned enforcement point, an extension of the existing
#       Category C mechanism, never a replacement for the backstop.
#
# WHY IT IS A PATCH AND NOT A COMMIT: guard.ts / the guard hook wiring is human-merge-only
#       (CLAUDE.md "Non-negotiables"; canonical list docs/security.md "Human-merge-only paths").
#       An autonomous worker's write to guard.ts/guard-hook.ts is denied at the tool level:
#         BLOCK [write-path] guard/reviewer/merge-driver source is human-merge-only
#       (guard.test.ts itself is NOT on that list — a direct `guardDecision("Write", ...)` probe
#       ALLOWs it, same as any other ordinary test file — but its own hunk below only makes
#       sense alongside the guard.ts/guard-hook.ts hunks it tests, so it ships in the same patch
#       rather than as a separate normal commit.) The guard constrains Claude tool calls, never
#       a human's editor.
#
# APPLY (from the repo root), then delete BOTH this file AND the apply-check test that exists
# only to prove it in CI pre-apply (its job is done the moment the patch below lands for real —
# leaving it in place afterward would either dangle on a deleted patch file or, if made to
# tolerate that, silently stop verifying an accidental deletion of the real deny rule):
#       git apply docs/patches/679-guard-default-branch-push.patch
#       git rm docs/patches/679-guard-default-branch-push.patch
#       git rm engine/src/guard/patch-679-apply-check.test.ts
#       npm --workspace engine test    # exercises the new BLOCK/ALLOW rows this patch adds
#
# KNOWN, DISCLOSED TRADE-OFF: the alias-injection check in (b) is intentionally unconditional —
# `git -c alias.p=push status` (an alias DEFINED but never actually invoked by this particular
# command line) is also blocked, even though that specific invocation is harmless on its own.
# This is the same "cannot prove it's safe, so block it" stance as (a)/(c), applied to the one
# case where proving safety would require resolving what the alias itself does. Similarly, (a)/
# (c)'s `$`/backtick/`*` characters are, in principle, legal (if bizarre) in a git ref name per
# `git check-ref-format` — this patch accepts that theoretical over-block in exchange for closing
# a demonstrated, executable bypass; this engine's own lane branches never use these characters
# (`lane-<issue>-<random>`, docs/security.md), so no real lane push is affected.
#
# ACCEPTED RESIDUAL (PM ruling, gate② round 2, item (8) above): a git-STATE-resolved alias — a
# pre-persisted `git config alias.*` or `GIT_CONFIG_*` environment alias, as opposed to an
# argv-local `-c`/`--config` injection — is out of scope for this argv-scanning guard, same class
# as checkControlSentinelArg's hardcoded-path residual. GitHub branch protection (DR #616) is the
# backstop of record for it. See docs/security.md's #679 section for the full statement.
#
# VERIFIED: this diff applies cleanly to main at ada7d4e (`git apply --check`, this issue's own
